Q: Suppress NOTHING_TO_INLINE warning globally A: <p>As of Kotlin 2.2.0 you can <a href="https://kotlinlang.org/docs/whatsnew21.html#global-warning-suppression" rel="nofollow noreferrer">suppress warnings globally</a> by adding a compiler flag:</p>
<pre class="lang-kotlin prettyprint-override"><code>// build.gradle.kts

kotlin {
    compilerOptions {
        freeCompilerArgs.add(&quot;-Xwarning-level=NOTHING_TO_INLINE:disabled&quot;)
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>This will suppress the compiler warning project-wide.</p>

Q: How to access each word from a std::split string by comma A: <p><code>std::string_view::data()</code> is not a C null-terminated string and can't be passed to a C function that expects a null at a string end. Sometimes, there is alternative C API that takes a string length. Particularly for <code>printf</code>, such API exists and we don't need to allocate C string buffers and copy string view data:</p>
<pre><code>  for (const auto&amp; currentWord : splitString) {
    printf(&quot;C:%.*s\n&quot;, static_cast&lt;int&gt;(currentWord.size()),
           currentWord.data());
  }
</code></pre>
<p>Note <code>.*</code> - it expects an outputs width, <code>int</code> historically.</p>

Q: Reducing casting and delegation boilerplate when using derived classes with generic params A: <p>One way to do this kind of double dispatch is to use the <code>dynamic</code> keyword. A word of caution, though:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>dynamic</code> does use reflection, so it could be slow.  Modern C# does cache some of the reflection so it's usually not too bad. Benchmark to be sure!</li>
<li>This sacrifices type safety, so you could get errors at runtime rather than at compile time if you misspell something. This is mitigated by the fact that the <code>dynamic</code> calls and the methods that they call are all in the same class, which makes it less likely to make a mistake.</li>
<li>You should not normally use <code>dynamic</code> in a public interface because it tends to spread if you do that. Here, though, it is only used in the implementation.</li>
</ul>
<p>The big advantage of this approach is that each herbivore is responsible for the kinds of food that it eats, and you can add a new kind of food without modifying any other code.</p>
<p>In the example code below, the <code>eatInternal(IVegetable vegetable)</code> implementation could throw a &quot;Not supported&quot; kind of exception, but I have chosen to just write something to the console.</p>
<p>The code below is also <a href="https://dotnetfiddle.net/jZJN67" rel="nofollow noreferrer">on .NET Fiddle</a>:</p>
<pre><code>using System;

namespace ConsoleApp1;

public interface IVegetable {}

public interface IHerbivore
{
    string Name { get; }
    void Eat(IVegetable vegetable);
}

public class Rabbit(string name): IHerbivore
{
    public string Name { get; } = name;

    public void Eat(IVegetable vegetable)
    {
        eatInternal((dynamic)vegetable);
    }

    // Rabbits eat carrots and lettuces.
    private void eatInternal(Carrot     carrot)    =&gt; Console.WriteLine($&quot;Rabbit {Name} is eating a carrot&quot;);
    private void eatInternal(Lettuce    lettuce)   =&gt; Console.WriteLine($&quot;Rabbit {Name} is eating lettuce&quot;);
    private void eatInternal(IVegetable vegetable) =&gt; Console.WriteLine($&quot;Rabbit {Name} is eating some unknown vegetable&quot;);
}

public class Donkey(string name) : IHerbivore
{
    public string Name { get; } = name;

    public void Eat(IVegetable vegetable)
    {
        eatInternal((dynamic)vegetable);
    }

    // Donkeys eat carrots and acorns.
    private void eatInternal(Carrot     carrot)    =&gt; Console.WriteLine($&quot;Donkey {Name} is eating a carrot&quot;);
    private void eatInternal(Acorn      acorn)     =&gt; Console.WriteLine($&quot;Donkey {Name} is eating an acorn&quot;);
    private void eatInternal(IVegetable vegetable) =&gt; Console.WriteLine($&quot;Donkey {Name} is eating some unknown vegetable&quot;);
}

public class Squirrel(string name): IHerbivore
{
    public string Name { get; } = name;

    public void Eat(IVegetable vegetable)
    {
        // Use double dynamic dispatch - no type switching needed
        eatInternal((dynamic)vegetable);
    }

    // Squirrels eat only acorns. 
    private void eatInternal(Acorn      acorn)     =&gt; Console.WriteLine($&quot;Squirrel {Name} is eating an acorn&quot;);
    private void eatInternal(IVegetable vegetable) =&gt; Console.WriteLine($&quot;Squirrel {Name} is eating some unknown vegetable&quot;);
}

public class Carrot : IVegetable {}
public class Lettuce: IVegetable {}
public class Acorn:   IVegetable {}
public class Banana:  IVegetable {} // Nothing eats bananas (no monkeys here!)

public static class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        IHerbivore[] herbivores = [new Rabbit(&quot;Bigwig&quot;), new Donkey(&quot;Eeyore&quot;), new Squirrel(&quot;Nutkin&quot;)];
        IVegetable[] vegetables = [new Carrot(), new Lettuce(), new Acorn(), new Banana()];

        foreach (var herbivore in herbivores)
        {
            foreach (var vegetable in vegetables)
            {
                herbivore.Eat(vegetable);
            }
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>The output from this is:</p>
<pre><code>Rabbit Bigwig is eating a carrot
Rabbit Bigwig is eating lettuce
Rabbit Bigwig is eating some unknown vegetable
Rabbit Bigwig is eating some unknown vegetable
Donkey Eeyore is eating a carrot
Donkey Eeyore is eating some unknown vegetable
Donkey Eeyore is eating an acorn
Donkey Eeyore is eating some unknown vegetable
Squirrel Nutkin is eating some unknown vegetable
Squirrel Nutkin is eating some unknown vegetable
Squirrel Nutkin is eating an acorn
Squirrel Nutkin is eating some unknown vegetable
</code></pre>

Q: Spring Boot Batch 6.x: Job Parameters are null in Reader despite using @StepScope A: <p>I debugged the issue and found the root cause in
<code>org.springframework.batch.core.launch.support.SimpleJobOperator</code>.</p>
<p>When using <code>JobOperator.start(Job job, JobParameters jobParameters)</code>, if the Job is configured with a JobParametersIncrementer, Spring Batch ignores the provided parameters and starts the next JobInstance instead.</p>
<p>This behavior is implemented in SimpleJobOperator:</p>
<pre><code>public JobExecution start(Job job, JobParameters jobParameters)
        throws JobInstanceAlreadyCompleteException,
               JobExecutionAlreadyRunningException,
               JobRestartException,
               InvalidJobParametersException {

    Assert.notNull(job, &quot;The Job must not be null.&quot;);
    Assert.notNull(jobParameters, &quot;The JobParameters must not be null.&quot;);

    if (job.getJobParametersIncrementer() != null) {
        if (!jobParameters.isEmpty() &amp;&amp; logger.isWarnEnabled()) {
            logger.warn(String.format(
                &quot;Attempting to launch job: [%s] which defines an incrementer with additional parameters: [%s]. &quot; +
                &quot;Additional parameters will be ignored.&quot;,
                job.getName(), jobParameters));
        }
        return startNextInstance(job);
    }

    return run(job, jobParameters);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Because my job was configured with a RunIdIncrementer, every call to <code>JobOperator.start(...)</code> ignored my parameters, resulting in unexpected behavior and the job appearing to run without parameters.</p>
<p>Fix</p>
<p>I removed the incrementer from the job configuration:</p>
<pre><code>@Bean
public Job wso2InstanceJob() {
    return new JobBuilder(&quot;wso2-instances-job&quot;, jobRepository)
            .preventRestart()
            .start(wso2InstanceStep())
            .build();
}
</code></pre>
<p>After removing the incrementer, the job runs correctly with the parameters passed to <code>JobOperator.start(...)</code>.</p>
<p>Hope this helps someone facing the same issue 👍</p>
